<h2 id="what-this-is">What this is, and what it is not</h2>

<p>
    This is not Playwright. Playwright drives a real browser from a Node process
    over a debugging protocol, and nothing in a web page can do that — so a
    portfolio that claims to run Playwright in your browser is claiming something
    that cannot happen.
</p>

<p>
    What runs here is a small test runner I wrote for this page. It implements
    the locator and auto-waiting semantics Playwright uses — <code>getByRole</code>
    with accessible-name matching, strict mode, retrying assertions, real
    timeouts — against a demo app in a same-origin iframe. The test code you
    read and edit below is the test code you would write in a real suite. The
    engine underneath it is not the real engine.
</p>

<p>
    The claim that it behaves the same is checkable rather than asserted.
    <code>tests/lab-parity.spec.js</code> runs every challenge through real
    Playwright against the same demo app and asserts the same outcome this
    runner produces. If the two ever disagree, the build goes red and the
    sentence above stops being true until someone fixes it. That suite is the
    receipt, and it is the part of this page I would want reviewed.
</p>

<h2 id="the-demo-app">The demo app</h2>

<p>
    An answer-review console: a generated answer, two scores, and a reviewer who
    approves or flags it. It is shaped like the systems in the case studies so
    the tests are about the domain I work in. Everything in it is invented —
    no production messages, prompts, retrieved content or dataset material, and
    it is not a depiction of any client system.
</p>

<p>
    It has four variants, and the challenges switch between them: a normal one, a
    <strong>flaky</strong> one whose review takes anywhere from 120ms to 900ms to
    load, a <strong>renamed</strong> one where a button's label changed, and an
    <strong>unnamed</strong> one where a control has no accessible name at all.
</p>

