<h2 id="how-the-runner-works">How the runner works</h2>

<p>
    A locator is a description, not an element. Nothing is queried when you call
    <code>getByRole</code>; the query runs when an action or an assertion asks
    for the element, and it keeps running every 50ms until the element turns up
    or the deadline passes. That is the whole reason a Playwright test does not
    need sleeps, and it is why the second challenge is solved by replacing four
    lines with one.
</p>

<p>
    Actions wait for more than existence. <code>click()</code> waits until the
    element is visible <em>and</em> enabled, which is Playwright's actionability
    check. My first version only waited for visible, so it clicked the demo
    app's approve button while it was still disabled and the test failed two
    lines later with no clue why. The parity suite caught that on its first run
    — real Playwright passed, this runner did not — and that divergence is the
    best argument I have for why the suite exists.
</p>

<p>
    Assertions retry the same way. <code>expect(locator).toHaveText("ready")</code>
    is not a comparison — it is a wait with a comparison attached. Getting that
    wrong would make the flake challenge passable by a sleep and unpassable by
    the correct answer, which is exactly backwards, so the parity suite checks it.
</p>

<p>
    Accessible names are computed the shallow way: <code>aria-label</code>, then
    <code>aria-labelledby</code>, then the associated label, then text content.
    It is not the full accessible-name algorithm and does not pretend to be. It
    is enough for the fourth challenge to make its point, which is that a
    role-based locator is an accessibility assertion wearing a different hat.
</p>
